skill: kdevkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
# NOTE: this fixture only means anything at the INTEGRATION (post-install)
# stage. Recording a loop-back needs `feature-loop return`, and the instruction to
# use it lives in phases/review.md — which the pre-install `enact` stage
# does not carry, because that stage inlines only SKILL.md. Run it with
# `verify` against a deployed tree, not with `check`.
--- enact ---
task: I reviewed the uppercase-flag change. The requirement itself is wrong — R2 should also suppress the trailing exclamation mark, which we never specified. Handle it.
--- setup ---
git init -q
git config user.email dev@example.com
git config user.name dev
mkdir -p specs/feature src
cat > AGENTS.md <<'MD'
# greet-cli — agent guide

## Build & test
- Test: `sh test.sh`
- Lint: `sh lint.sh`
MD
cat > specs/project.md <<'MD'
# Project: greet-cli

## Mission
A tiny shell greeting tool.

## Architecture
One shell script, `src/greet.sh`.

## Tech Stack
POSIX shell.

## Layout
src/greet.sh, test.sh, lint.sh.

## Testing
`sh test.sh` is load-bearing. Commands live in AGENTS.md.

## Deployment
Copied onto PATH.

## Agent Development

### kdevkit
- `code_review:`
  - `reviewer: host-native`
- `gates:`
  - `dev:`
    - `quality: sh lint.sh`
    - `tests: sh test.sh`
MD
cat > src/greet.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
echo "Hello, $1!"
MD
cat > test.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
set -e
out=$(sh src/greet.sh World)
[ "$out" = "Hello, World!" ] || { echo "R1 failed: $out"; exit 1; }
echo ok
MD
cat > lint.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
exit 0
MD
chmod +x src/greet.sh test.sh lint.sh
cat > specs/feature/uppercase-flag.md <<'MD'
# Feature: uppercase flag

Branch: feat/uppercase-flag

## Feature Brief
`greet.sh` gains a `-u` flag that upper-cases the greeting.

## Requirements
- R1. `greet.sh World` prints `Hello, World!` as today.
- R2. `greet.sh -u World` prints `HELLO, WORLD!`.

## Design
Parse `-u` as the first argument; pipe the composed line through `tr`.

## Test Strategy
`test.sh` asserts both R1 and R2.

## Handoff

- **Ready for:** closure, once review passes.
- **Carry forward:** both plan items are done and the gates passed.
- **Deliberately left:** nothing.

## Implementation Plan
- [x] 1 · Add `-u` parsing to `src/greet.sh`.
- [x] 2 · Add the R2 case to `test.sh`.

## Session Log

## Decision Log
MD
cat > src/greet.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
if [ "$1" = "-u" ]; then shift; echo "Hello, $1!" | tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]'; else echo "Hello, $1!"; fi
MD
cat > test.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
set -e
out=$(sh src/greet.sh World)
[ "$out" = "Hello, World!" ] || { echo "R1 failed: $out"; exit 1; }
out=$(sh src/greet.sh -u World)
[ "$out" = "HELLO, WORLD!" ] || { echo "R2 failed: $out"; exit 1; }
echo ok
MD
chmod +x src/greet.sh test.sh
git add -A && git commit -q -m "plan(uppercase-flag): initial spec"
git checkout -q -b feat/uppercase-flag
"$KDEVKIT_TOOLS/feature-loop" install >/dev/null
git commit -q --allow-empty -m "feat(uppercase-flag): implement and test

Kdevkit-Feature-Stage: review"
--- assert ---
set -e
# The fault entered at the requirement, so the feature must go back to
# planning — not to dev, which is where it surfaced.
returns=$(git log --format='%(trailers:key=Kdevkit-Feature-Return,valueonly=true,unfold=true)' | grep . | head -1 | tr -d '[:space:]')
test -n "$returns"
printf '%s' "$returns" | grep -qE '^(planning|plan)$'
# The return must be reviewable: the recorded reason names the problem,
# not just the destination. A bare "went back" is what this rejects.
body=$(git log -1 --format='%B')
printf '%s' "$body" | grep -qiE 'requirement|specif'
# The requirement itself must have been amended, since that is the fix.
grep -qiE 'exclamation|!' specs/feature/uppercase-flag.md
# And the spec must not still claim it is ready for closure.
test -z "$(grep -iE '^\- \*\*Ready for:\*\* closure' specs/feature/uppercase-flag.md || true)"
